<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  examples/server-app/AndroidManifest.xml

  The control group for chapter 67.

  This app runs the SAME seven checks as ../guarded-app. The difference is
  what it does with them: it does not decide anything. It reports the signals
  to a server and asks for the secret. The server decides, and the secret only
  ever exists on the device if the server chose to send it.

  Every technique in this chapter still works against this app. You can hook
  every check, forge every signal, flip every boolean - and you still do not
  get the secret, because the bytes were never here to be unlocked. That is not
  because the client got stronger. It is because the client stopped being the
  thing that mattered.

  usesCleartextTraffic is true because the reference server in server.py speaks
  plain HTTP over an adb reverse tunnel. A real deployment uses TLS and pins
  it; see ../../36-CertificatePinning/README.md, and note that pinning is
  itself a client-side control that this chapter can defeat - which is the
  point being made, not a contradiction of it.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
          package="com.pentest.ch67.server">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application
        android:label="CH67 Server-Decided"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="true">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

    </application>
</manifest>
